  <!-- 5. WHY THIS MATTERS + 3-STEP SETUP -->
  <section class="band art-band" id="details" style="--band-art:url('/art/ownership.png'); --band-tone:var(--tone-indigo)">
    <div class="band-art" role="img" aria-label="Background: Schip in nood bij storm op zee (ship in distress in a storm), Johannes Hilverdink, 1861"></div>
    <div class="container wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>Your agent outlives any one provider.</h2>
        <p>Most AI products store your agent's memory on their servers. Baken stores it on yours, so a pricing change, a policy change or a retired model does not take your agent with it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="clean-grid">
        <div class="clean-item">
          <h3>Memory is plain files.</h3>
          <p>Everything the agent learns is saved as readable text files on your computer. You can open, edit, version or back them up like any other files.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="clean-item">
          <h3>Models are swappable.</h3>
          <p>Because memory lives outside the model, you can switch to a different model at any time. The agent keeps its history and behaviour.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="clean-item">
          <h3>You run the host.</h3>
          <p>Baken needs a host machine running letta-code and the Baken wrapper, set up as a service on Linux or Windows. Then install the Android or desktop app and scan the pairing code. No account is required. There is no macOS or iOS build yet.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="clean-item">
          <h3>No engagement tricks.</h3>
          <p>There are no streaks, no subscription and no re-engagement notifications. The app is a client for your agent, not a place to spend time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. FAQ -->
  <section class="band band-rule" id="faq">
    <div class="container wrap faq-container">
      <div class="band-head centered">
        <h2>Questions</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-list">
        <details open>
          <summary>What happens if Baken stops being updated tomorrow?</summary>
          <p>Your agent keeps working. The source is published on GitHub (<a href="/licenses">source available</a>), it runs without contacting any server we operate, and its memory is in plain text files on your disk.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is Baken free?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Baken is free, and the source is published on GitHub (<a href="/licenses">source available</a>). Local models cost nothing to run. If you use a cloud model, you pay that provider directly.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need my own computer?</summary>
          <p>Yes. The agent runs on a computer or server you control, and the phone app connects to it. This is what keeps memory and files on your hardware.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What happens when I switch to a newer AI model?</summary>
          <p>Nothing is lost. Memory and conversation history are stored on your machine, not inside any model provider, so the new model continues from the same state.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Who can see my conversations?</summary>
          <p>There is no Baken account and no central database. Your phone and computer connect directly, using a key that only those two devices hold. If you use a cloud model, that provider sees the requests you send it.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. THE "WHY BAKEN" ORIGIN & INTERACTIVE TRANSIT (MOVED TO THE END AS THE SIGNATURE CODA) -->
  <section class="band band-rule band-tinted why-chart" id="why-baken">
    <div class="why-chart-bg" id="chartPlate" role="img" aria-label="Background: Paskaart van het Vlie en de Marsdiep, 1583, by Lucas Jansz. Waghenaer, engraved by Joannes van Doetechum, showing the Wadden Sea channels between Texel, Vlieland and Terschelling."></div>
    <div class="container wrap why-chart-inner">
      <div class="origin-grid">
        <div class="origin-copy">
          <h2>Why it's called Baken.</h2>
          <p>
            The sandbanks of the Dutch Wadden Sea move with every tide, so a chart drawn in spring could be wrong by autumn. To navigate it, islanders on Texel, Vlieland and Terschelling built <em>bakens</em>: fixed landmarks of stone, iron and timber set into the dunes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Bakens were built in pairs. A sailor who lined up the front mark with the taller rear mark, a sightline called a <em>kenning</em>, was in the safe channel regardless of where the sand had moved.
          </p>
          <p>
            Models and providers change in a similar way. Baken is meant to be the fixed reference: your computer holds the agent's memory, and your phone stays aligned with it wherever you are.
          </p>
          <p class="origin-cta">
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href={LINKS.download} data-umami-event="download-why">Download Baken</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Interactive Wadden Sea Transit Instrument at the end of the story -->
        <div class="origin-instrument dark-surface" id="transitInstrument">
          <div class="transit-meta">
            <span>FIG. I — DE KENNING (WADDEN SEA TRANSIT)</span>
            <span class="transit-readout" id="transitReadout">Bearing aligned · 0.0° drift</span>
          </div>

          <svg class="transit-svg" id="transitSvg" viewBox="0 0 560 220" aria-label="Interactive seamark alignment">
            <defs>
              <radialGradient id="transitGlow" cx="50%" cy="52%" r="48%">
                <stop offset="0%" stop-color="#EE5A3F" stop-opacity="0.46" />
                <stop offset="60%" stop-color="#EE5A3F" stop-opacity="0.11" />
                <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#EE5A3F" stop-opacity="0" />
              </radialGradient>
            </defs>

            <path class="contour c1" d="M -20 176 C 140 124, 290 198, 450 144 C 510 124, 550 148, 580 136" />
            <path class="contour c2" d="M -20 142 C 120 92, 310 166, 470 112 C 520 96, 555 118, 580 104" />
            <path class="contour c3" d="M -20 106 C 150 58, 330 132, 480 80 C 530 64, 560 88, 580 74" />

            <line id="sightRay" x1="280" y1="12" x2="280" y2="208" stroke="rgba(238,90,63,0.75)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4" />
            <circle id="sightGlow" cx="280" cy="112" r="76" fill="url(#transitGlow)" />

            <g id="rearStone" transform="translate(202, 24) scale(0.78)">
              <path d={BAKEN_ANCHOR} fill="#F2F2F5" />
            </g>
            <g id="frontStone" transform="translate(202, 24) scale(0.78)">
              <path d={BAKEN_PEBBLE} fill="#EE5A3F" />
            </g>

            <text x="18" y="204" fill="#8C8C96" font-size="10.5" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">REAR · YOUR COMPUTER</text>
            <text x="392" y="204" fill="#EE5A3F" font-size="10.5" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">FRONT · YOUR PHONE</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="why-chart-cap">
        Chart: <em>Paskaart van het Vlie en de Marsdiep</em>, 1583 · L. J. Waghenaer, engr. J. van Doetechum · Rijksmuseum NG-43
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

